Elhage et al., "Toy Models of Superposition" (Anthropic, 2022) — https://transformer-circuits.pub/2022/toy_model/index.html — the paper that explains why neural network features are hard to find (superposition of more features than dimensions), foundational to the whole interpretability agenda.
Bricken et al., "Towards Monosemanticity: Decomposing Language Models With Dictionary Learning" (Anthropic, 2023) — https://transformer-circuits.pub/2023/monosemantic-features/index.html — first demonstration that sparse autoencoders can pull genuinely interpretable, monosemantic features out of a real language model. This is the most concrete "here's actually what's happening inside a mind-like system" progress that exists right now, computational or biological.
Elhage et al., "A Mathematical Framework for Transformer Circuits" (Anthropic, 2021) — https://transformer-circuits.pub/2021/framework/index.html — treats attention/MLP computation as literal linear-algebraic circuits you can trace end to end; genuinely rigorous, not metaphorical.
Trinh, Wu, Le, He, Luong, "Solving Olympiad Geometry Without Human Demonstrations" (AlphaGeometry, Nature, 2024) — DOI: 10.1038/s41586-023-06747-5 — AI generating actual new synthetic-geometry proofs at IMO gold-medal level, via a neuro-symbolic combination rather than pure LLM guessing. This is the clearest case so far of AI producing genuinely new mathematical reasoning, not retrieval.
Romera-Paredes et al., "Mathematical Discoveries from Program Search with Large Language Models" (FunSearch, Nature, 2023) — DOI: 10.1038/s41586-023-06924-6 — found a new construction improving the best-known bound on the cap set problem, a real open combinatorics question. Same theme: AI as a genuine mathematical collaborator, not just an assistant.
Amari, "Information Geometry and Its Applications" (Springer, 2016; earlier foundational papers from the 1980s-90s) — the mathematical toolkit (Fisher information metric, dual connections on statistical manifolds) that's quietly become the shared language across statistics, ML optimization theory, and — relevantly to this whole thread — some of the more careful mathematical-consciousness-science formalizations. No single canonical arXiv link since the field spans decades; search "Amari information geometry" for the standard textbook.
Stoudenmire & Schwab, "Supervised Learning with Quantum-Inspired Tensor Networks" (2016) — https://arxiv.org/abs/1605.05775 — genuinely uses the same matrix-product-state math from the holography/tensor-network cluster I gave you earlier, applied directly to machine learning. This is the real, technical bridge between the quantum-gravity tensor-network program and AI — not vibes, actual shared formalism.
Preskill, "Quantum Computing in the NISQ Era and Beyond" (2018) — https://arxiv.org/abs/1801.00862 — still the clearest honest assessment of what near-term quantum computers can and can't do; useful precisely because it's a rigorous down-call on hype, from the person who coined "quantum supremacy."
Arute et al., "Quantum Supremacy Using a Programmable Superconducting Processor" (Google, Nature 2019) — DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1666-5 — the actual experimental demonstration of a quantum speedup on a contrived task; worth reading with Preskill's caveats in mind since the practical-usefulness gap is still large.
Kaplan et al., "Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models" (2020) — https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.08361 — the empirical-physics-style paper (power laws, not architecture-specific tricks) that predicted how model performance scales with size/data/compute, and set off the whole current era of AI progress. If you want one paper that's actually reshaped a field's trajectory in the last five years, this is a strong candidate.